# Debugging and Development Tools: Finding and Fixing Bugs Like a Professional

> Bugs are the normal byproduct of writing software, not failures
> of character — professionals differ from beginners mainly in their
> toolkit: debuggers, logs, version control, and tests.

## 1. A Field Guide to Bugs

| Kind | When it bites | Tiny example |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Syntax error** | Before the program runs at all | `if x == 5` missing its colon |
| **Runtime error** | Mid-execution, crashes it | `10 / 0` → ZeroDivisionError |
| **Logic error** | Never crashes — wrong answer | averaging with `total * count` |

Syntax errors surface at **compile time**; runtime and logic errors
appear only when code executes — a program that runs is not the same
as a program that is right. The nastiest bugs are logic errors,
because nothing complains; only tests and careful reading catch them.

## 2. The Debugging Mindset

Professional debugging is a loop, not guesswork:

@diagram tree id="debug-loop" layout="tree" aria="The debugging cycle from reproducing the bug to adding a regression test"
  "Reproduce the bug" -> "Isolate the smallest case"
  "Isolate the smallest case" -> "Hypothesize the cause"
  "Hypothesize the cause" -> "Test the hypothesis"
  "Test the hypothesis" -> "Fix and verify"
  "Fix and verify" -> "Add a regression test"
@/diagram

[definition]
**Bisecting** is the systematic version of guessing: halve the
remaining suspicious range each time, so a million possibilities take
about twenty probes. `git bisect` applies the idea to commits.
[/definition]

**Rubber-duck debugging**: explain the code line by line to a duck
(or colleague) — stating the expectation aloud exposes where reality
disagrees.

## 3. Using a Debugger

A **debugger** pauses your program mid-flight — a microscope for
running code.

| Feature | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| **Breakpoint** | Pause execution when a chosen line runs |
| **Step over** | Execute the next line without entering functions it calls |
| **Step into** | Dive inside the next function call |
| **Watch** | Track a variable or expression as you step |
| **Call stack** | The chain of calls that got you here |

[important]
Read a **stack trace** top frame first: it is where the error actually
happened; below it sit the callers, back to who opened the door.
[/important]

## 4. Logging vs Print Debugging

Scattering `print("here1")` works, but you must remove it and cannot
filter by severity. **Logging** is print's professional sibling:
permanent, level-tagged, filterable without touching code.

| Level | Meaning | Example use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `DEBUG` | Fine-grained internal detail | Variable values in a loop |
| `INFO` | Normal milestones | "server started on port 8080" |
| `WARNING` | Something suspicious, still working | Retry succeeded, slowly |
| `ERROR` | An operation failed | Payment could not be processed |

## 5. Version Control with Git

**Version control** records every committed snapshot of your project
— who, what, when, and why — and lets any past state return.

```text
git clone URL      # copy a remote repository
git add cart.js    # stage changes for the next commit
git commit -m "Fix off-by-one in cart total"
git push           # share your commits
git pull           # fetch and merge others' commits
```

**Branches** isolate work without disturbing the main line; a
**merge conflict** arises only when two branches edit the same lines,
and Git asks you to pick. A `.gitignore` keeps build output,
dependencies, and secrets out of history.

[tip]
Commit small and often: each commit is a reviewable, revertible unit,
and the history itself becomes documentation.
[/tip]

## 6. The Testing Pyramid

| Layer | Scope | Speed | Quantity | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Unit** | One function or class, isolated | Milliseconds | Thousands | **Mocks** replace collaborators (databases, clocks) |
| **Integration** | Several real pieces together | Seconds | Hundreds | Talks to a test database or API |
| **End-to-end** | The whole system through the real UI | Minutes | Tens | Closest to the user, flakiest and slowest |

## 7. TDD and Assertions

**Test-driven development (TDD)** writes the test *before* the code,
in a three-beat rhythm:

[example]
**Red** — write a small failing test describing the behavior you
want. **Green** — make it pass with the simplest code. **Refactor** —
clean up test and code, keeping the suite green. Repeat.
[/example]

An **assertion** checks a claim: `assert total == 42` fails loudly if
false — the building block of every test.

## 8. Getting Unstuck

| Practice | The habit |
| --- | --- |
| Read the error message | Actually read it — top frame first; it names file and line |
| Timebox | Struggle deliberately for ~30–45 minutes, then seek help |
| Ask well | State goal, expected vs actual, what you tried, versions, minimal example |

[warning]
Debugging by random edits is not debugging: without a reproducible
bug, you cannot know the fix worked — or that nothing else broke.
[/warning]

## Quick Revision

| Concept | One-line recall |
| --- | --- |
| Bug taxonomy | Syntax (before running), runtime (crash), logic (silent wrong) |
| Debug loop | Reproduce → isolate → hypothesize → test → fix → regression-test |
| Stack trace | Top frame first: where it happened |
| Git rhythm | clone, add, commit, push, pull; branch to isolate |
| Pyramid | Many fast unit tests; few slow end-to-end tests |
